# Customer-name denylist for evals/check_eval_hygiene.py (APPSEC-3971).
#
# Each non-comment line is the lowercase SHA-256 hash of ONE forbidden
# lowercase token. The scanner hashes every word token in the corpus —
# plus adjacent 2-/3-token joins and digit-stripped variants, so
# "acme corp", "acme-corp", "api.acme-corp.example.net" and
# "acmecorp2024" all reduce to the same candidate as "acmecorp" — and
# fails the build on a match.
#
# THREAT-MODEL LIMIT — read before trusting this: these are UNSALTED
# hashes of low-entropy names. Anyone holding a candidate list (e.g. a
# customer roster) can reverse every entry by brute force in seconds,
# and the entry count itself reveals how many names are considered
# sensitive. What this file buys is grep-resistance — the names never
# appear in the repo in searchable form — NOT cryptographic secrecy.
#
# To add an entry (macOS/Linux):
#
#     printf '%s' 'name' | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z' | shasum -a 256
#
# Rules for entries:
#   - Hash the name in ALL LOWERCASE (the scanner lowercases before
#     hashing; a hash of a mixed-case string will never match).
#   - Strip separators AND digits: hash "acmecorp", not "acme-corp" or
#     "acmecorp2024" — the scanner reduces corpus tokens to that
#     canonical form before comparing. Multi-word names need one entry
#     per identifying word plus the joined form.
#   - Known residual gaps (see evals/HYGIENE.md): a name fused with
#     other letters ("acmecorpinc"), leetspeak substitutions, and
#     homoglyph spellings will NOT match exact hashing.
#   - Append a short comment ABOVE the hash saying who added it and when
#     (never what it hashes).
#
# Example entry (the placeholder token "examplecustomer" — safe to keep,
# useful for testing the mechanism end-to-end):
2e4ef3764e13f4c891b3529546c2043c7c9a1cf97e6310050b159cff1693ba8d
